fact WorkItem(id: "WI-658", description: some(value: "Enforce the `Eq` reflexivity law `eq_refl` PER-INSTANCE at LOAD time -- the instance-law conformance check WI-644 promised but never delivered. A `provides Eq[T=X]` / `fact Eq[T=X]` whose carrier X can REFUTE `eq_refl: eq(?a,?a) <=> true` must FAIL to load. Canonical case: IEEE Float -- `eq(nan,nan)=false` refutes reflexivity, so `provides Eq[T=Float]` must be rejected (Float lawfully provides only PartialEq/PartialOrd). Lawful carriers (Int64/BigInt/String/Bool, and the TotalFloat newtype) discharge `eq_refl` and keep loading.\n\nWHY THIS EXISTS -- ORPHANED ENFORCEMENT. WI-644's ACCEPTANCE requires \"PartialEq/Eq/PartialOrd/Ord load with eq_refl a CHECKED law\" and \"a non-reflexive `provides Eq[T=Float]` FAILS to load\"; its description asserts \"Float does NOT provide -- the instance-law conformance checker rejects it automatically.\" That enforcement was DEFERRED and then MIS-ATTRIBUTED to WI-650. But WI-650 was REPURPOSED: its delivered commit 31aec94b drops Map's O(n^2) relational eq apparatus and guards an unbacked Map.eq override -- its diff touches only stdlib/anthill/prelude/map.anthill, kb/typing.rs, and Map tests; NOTHING about eq_refl / reflexivity / Float. So the per-instance eq_refl discharge currently has NO OWNER. The stale pointers must be repointed HERE: the project memory (wi644-partial-total-eq-ordering) and the code comment in rustland/anthill-stl/anthill/float.anthill both still say \"auto-refuting a `provides Eq[Float]` is WI-650\" -- false.\n\nEMPIRICAL GAP (verified 2026-07-07): `anthill load` of a namespace asserting `fact Eq[T = Float]` (stdlib auto-included) loads CLEAN -- exit 0, \"loaded: 2076 facts, 106 rules\", no rejection. Today `eq_refl` (stdlib/anthill/prelude/eq.anthill:36) is a DORMANT obligation: it documents the contract but is never discharged/refuted per instance (eq.anthill:12-16 says so explicitly).\n\nMECHANISM (reuse existing machinery, no new language surface -- per WI-644's own \"NO new language machinery\" note): the instance-law CONFORMANCE CHECKING in kb/load.rs (Specialization / requires-law Discharged, ~3866-3972, ~12425) already discharges a spec's laws at a concrete provider. Extend it so that for each `provides/fact Eq[T=X]` the parent spec's law `eq_refl` is CHECKED at carrier X, and a positive REFUTATION rejects the provision.\n\nKEY DESIGN QUESTION -- how the checker obtains a refutation witness for an infinite carrier. You CANNOT enumerate to PROVE `for-all a. eq(a,a)`, so reject on positive REFUTATION ONLY, never on mere non-discharge (stay sound under WI-628 truncation-awareness -- never-decide-undecided). Options:\n  (a) Special-value probe: discharge `eq_refl` against the carrier's declared distinguished constants -- Float exposes `nan`/`infinity`/`negativeInfinity` as term-level consts (WI-084); `eq(nan,nan)` reduces to false via the delivered IEEE eval/resolver path (WI-644 step 2) => refuted. Cheapest; catches Float.\n  (b) Host non-reflexive MARKER: a host carrier binding declares itself non-reflexive (the Rust `f64: !Eq` analogue); the checker rejects `provides Eq` for a marked carrier. Simplest/most robust; needs no reduction.\n  (c) Proof-pass discharge (WI-558 prove-verify): run `eq_refl` as a proof obligation over the carrier; an IEEE-Float model yields the `nan` counterexample.\nRECOMMEND (a)+(b) -- probe distinguished consts AND honor an explicit host non-reflexive marker. DISCUSS before building.\n\nACCEPTANCE:\n - A synthetic non-reflexive `provides Eq[T=Float]` (or `fact Eq[T=Float]`) FAILS to load with a conformance / law-refutation diagnostic naming `eq_refl`.\n - `provides PartialEq[T=Float]` / `provides PartialOrd[T=Float]` still load (partial is lawful, IEEE).\n - `provides Eq[T=TotalFloat]` and the lawful primitives' `fact Eq[T=Int64/BigInt/String/Bool]` still load.\n - `eq_refl` becomes an ENFORCED obligation; update its comment in stdlib/anthill/prelude/eq.anthill (currently \"not yet enforced ... WI-650\") and the rustland/anthill-stl/anthill/float.anthill comment to reference THIS ticket.\n - Repro test (mirror wi645 naming) asserting the Float provision is rejected and TotalFloat/Int64 accepted; full workspace `cargo-test` green.\n - Sibling WI-649 (use-site `requires Eq[K]` => `Map[K=Float]` load error) is COMPLEMENTARY (provision-site here vs call-site there) and unaffected.\n\nUnblocks CLOSING WI-644 (this is one of its two outstanding enforcement guarantees; the other is WI-649). Builds on WI-644's already-merged CORE (eq_refl law + IEEE eval/resolver path) -- no open dependency, so no cycle with the parent. REF: stdlib/anthill/prelude/{eq,float,totalfloat}.anthill, rustland/anthill-stl/anthill/float.anthill, kb/load.rs conformance hooks, WI-644 (parent, blocked on this), WI-649 (sibling), WI-650 (number REPURPOSED away from this -- do NOT rely on it), WI-558 (prove-verify), WI-628 (sound truncation), WI-084 (Float consts), docs/proposals/library/004. Rust + scaland (scaland has no operation/law loading -- mirror only if it gains conformance checking)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-07T23:56:26Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-658", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED via option (d) — a `NonEq` spec + Float instance + a load-time `Eq` ⊥ `NonEq` mutual-exclusion check (`check_eq_noneq_exclusive` / `LoadError::IncompatibleEqNonEq`), NOT the a/b/c `eq_refl`-refutation menu the ticket originally proposed. RATIONALE: a probe showed integrity constraints are NOT load-enforced (a `:- ...` constraint never fires at load or `anthill check`), so the mutual-exclusion had to live in the provider conformance pass, not as a language constraint. Float `provides NonEq` (witness `nonEqRefl() = nan`); a user `provides Eq[Float]` is now a load error; opt-in — does nothing without a NonEq declaration (`eq_refl` stays dormant/documentation-only, it is NOT discharged). Regression test `wi658_eq_noneq_exclusive_test`. /code-review (3 agents, max effort): no correctness bug; fixed the stale `eq.anthill`/`float.anthill` comments that still described the abandoned eq_refl-discharge approach. CARRY-OVER to WI-664: the check keys on the carrier BASE symbol, so it must become binding-aware before parametric/composite carriers provide Eq at one instantiation and NonEq at another. Composite (entity / named-tuple) congruent Eq/NonEq derivation + the coupled `semantic_equal` structural-reflexivity laundering soundness fix (`eq(Point(nan,_),Point(nan,_))` currently launders to true) = WI-664 (depends on this).", at: "2026-07-07T23:56:25Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-658", author: "user", content: "POINTER REPAIR (WI-835, 2026-07-29): this ticket's last acceptance bullet and its REF list name 'sibling WI-649 (use-site requires Eq[K] => Map[K=Float] load error)' -- that number is WRONG. WI-649 is the reify/reify_value cyclic-sigma occurs-check ticket (Delivered); the number was REPURPOSED -- the same failure mode this ticket was filed to fix on the provision-site half, which had been mis-attributed to the repurposed WI-650. The use-site half is WI-835 (Delivered 2026-07-29): every written parameterized type instantiation is recorded at the sole type lowering and refused post-eq_derive when a requires Eq[param] binding names a NonEq carrier. Read every 'WI-649' in this ticket as WI-835. The complementarity the bullet asserts is real and holds: WI-658 refuses an unlawful PROVISION (Eq exclusive-or NonEq at the carrier), WI-835 refuses an unlawful USE (a NonEq carrier bound where a sort requires Eq).", at: "2026-07-29T13:55:24Z")
